f2f9965bd350"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "These are the singularities of a curve: a double point, a stretch where the \
curve runs along itself, a point where it reverses. A walk without cusps is \
immersed (",
 Cell[BoxData[
  TemplateBox[{
    Cell[
     TextData["InfraImmersedQ"]], 
    "paclet:WolframInstitute/InfraGeometry/ref/InfraImmersedQ", 
    "WolframInstitute/InfraGeometry Package Symbol"},
   "PackageLink",
   BaseStyle->"InlineFormula"]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "76552461-4776-4ae1-b438-0b286294bb58"],
 "); one that also has no tangencies and only double points away from its \
ends is generic (",
 Cell[BoxData[
  TemplateBox[{
    Cell[
     TextData["InfraGenericQ"]], 
    "paclet:WolframInstitute/InfraGeometry/ref/InfraGenericQ", 
    "WolframInstitute/InfraGeometry Package Symbol"},
   "PackageLink",
   BaseStyle->"InlineFormula"]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "0386748c-4da1-445f-995c-2f3c9b8187b2"],
 ")."
}], "Notes",ExpressionUUID->"848a0462-4dc8-4313-bf53-e48fb777e8b7"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["walk", "TI"],
 " is a vertex list, a walk graph, or a list of walk graphs. A cycle graph is \
a closed walk, read round the cycle; its positions are taken modulo its \
length. A vertex list is always open: a list that ends where it starts counts \
its ends as a self-intersection."
}], "Notes",ExpressionUUID->"1074bb56-93be-4292-a6fe-86ebc65977c4"],

Cell["\<\
The graph is not needed: the singularities are read off the vertex sequence.\
\>", "Notes",ExpressionUUID->"7ae22123-56b3-41eb-a4bd-a737a712cae2"]
}, Open  ]],

